WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.090
You’ve never met your bank’s computers.

2
00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:04.730
You’ve never whispered a

3
00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:06.550
secret key to a chat app.

4
00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:09.610
So how do you talk to them in secret,

5
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.190
over a network anyone can listen to?

6
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.640
Today: locks and keys — how public-key

7
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:19.160
cryptography lets strangers share secrets.

8
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.960
With a simple cipher, both sides

9
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.780
need the same secret key.

10
00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.290
But how do you send that key?

11
00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:29.600
Anyone listening on the network

12
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.980
could copy it on the way.

13
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.650
Public-key cryptography solves it with

14
00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.790
two keys that belong together.

15
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.400
The public key is like an open

16
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:41.800
padlock: you give copies to everyone.

17
00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.600
The private key is the only key that

18
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.440
opens it, and it never leaves you.

19
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.580
Imagine handing out open

20
00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.080
padlocks to your friends.

21
00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.590
Anyone can put a note in a box

22
00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.330
and snap your padlock shut.

23
00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.840
Once it’s shut, only you can open it —

24
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.520
not even the friend who locked it.

25
00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.810
One famous method, RSA,

26
00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.850
rests on a simple fact.

27
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.770
Multiplying two primes is easy:

28
00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.030
sixty-one times fifty-three is three

29
00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:14.270
thousand two hundred and thirty-three.

30
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.180
Going back from the answer to the primes

31
00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:19.380
gets far harder as the numbers grow.

32
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.460
Real keys are about six hundred digits

33
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.020
long: all the computers on Earth today

34
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.380
couldn’t split one in a lifetime.

35
00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:28.270
Quick check!

36
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.560
I want to send you a secret.

37
00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.090
Which key do I lock it with?

38
00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:35.480
Your public key.

39
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.770
Anyone can lock with it, and only

40
00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.530
your private key opens it.

41
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.980
Now turn it around, and you get

42
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.420
something just as useful: a signature.

43
00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.700
To sign, you use your

44
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.180
private key on a message.

45
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.770
Anyone can check it with your public

46
00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.490
key — and since only your key could

47
00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.710
have made it, they know it’s from you,

48
00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.870
and that nobody changed a word.

49
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.640
Phone updates are signed like this,

50
00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.780
so a fake update gets refused.

51
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.670
When a website uses HTTPS, your browser

52
00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:12.330
checks the site’s certificate, agrees a

53
00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.350
secret key with it using public keys,

54
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.070
then encrypts everything.

55
00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:18.880
But the padlock only proves the

56
00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.580
connection is private, to that address.

57
00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.600
A scam site can have a padlock too.

58
00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:26.580
Your turn.

59
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.000
A page that asks for your mobile

60
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.000
money PIN shows a padlock.

61
00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.280
Can you trust it because of the padlock?

62
00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:38.350
No: scam sites get padlocks too.

63
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.860
Remember: the public key locks and checks,

64
00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:45.340
the private key opens and signs, and the

65
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:48.260
padlock means private, not honest.

66
00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.560
And defenders are already

67
00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.700
switching to new methods built to

68
00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.540
resist future quantum computers.

69
00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.460
Try it tomorrow: in the lab, make a

70
00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:01.720
tiny key pair, lock a word, then sign a

71
00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:03.480
message and change one letter.

72
00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.010
Then, in a chat app, find where you can

73
00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.890
compare security codes with a friend, and

74
00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:12.110
work out together what a match proves.
